@flexiarg operator/span-the-turn-boundary-by-chaining
@title Span The Turn Boundary By Chaining Parked Work
@status accepted 2026-11-11: proposed by 象 for 3 turns (Joe 2026-11-11: recurrence suffices); attested once used in a mission or a War Machine cascade
@provenance 象 proposals, ~/.emacs-graph/session-turn-analysis/<record>[.json].candidates.json; records: turn-EiWVid turn-Ev3IJ0 turn-PkpLp0; first proposed for: A delegated batch may exceed what one turn can hold.
@why [cycle-machine/step-machine]

! conclusion: Name the mechanism (parks) and the condition (too much for one turn) when delegating, so splitting is a declared plan rather than an improvised one.

  + IF:
    The work is a list of similar items and the agent has a park/resume mechanism.

  + HOWEVER:
    Without a sanctioned spanning mechanism, the agent either truncates the list silently or blows the turn trying to finish; both failures hide the overrun.

  + THEN:
    Name the mechanism (parks) and the condition (too much for one turn) when delegating, so splitting is a declared plan rather than an improvised one.

  + BECAUSE:
    The parked chain is the step machine seen from the operator's seat: intents parked with their state, resumed in order; the operator's move is to authorise and name that mechanism as the sanctioned span.
